  "actionResultText": "Here's your interactive tangent line explorer! 🎯\n\n---\n\n**How to use it:**\n1. **Drag the `a` slider** to move the point along the curve $f(x) = x^2$\n2. Watch the **tangent line rotate** as you move the point\n3. Check the **slope value** — it equals $2a$ at each position\n\n---\n\n**The key idea — 3 steps:**\n\n1. **The derivative** $f'(x)$ gives the *instantaneous slope* of a curve at any point.\n\n2. For $f(x) = x^2$, the derivative is:\n$$f'(x) = 2x$$\nSo at $x = a$, the slope is $2a$.\n\n3. The **tangent line** at point $(a,\\, a^2)$ uses that slope:\n$$y = 2a(x - a) + a^2$$\n(This is just point-slope form: $y - y_1 = m(x - x_1)$)\n\n---\n\n**Things to notice:**\n- At $x = 0$, the slope is **0** → tangent line is flat (it's the minimum!)\n- At $x = 1$, slope is **2** → line tilts up\n- At $x = -2$, slope is **-4** → line tilts steeply down\n\nThe derivative is literally just **\"how tilted is the tangent line?\"** at each point. Want me to go deeper into any part of this?",
